TI  - In-capillary enzymatic digestion beyond trypsin for the sensitive targeted 
      bottom-up analysis of protein biomarkers by capillary electrophoresis-mass 
      spectrometry.
PG  - 344497
LID - S0003-2670(25)00891-8 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.aca.2025.344497 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Targeted bottom-up proteomics is of great interest for the 
      straightforward, accurate, and sensitive measurement of specific protein 
      biomarkers from surrogate peptide fragments. However, this approach typically 
      relies on off-line enzymatic digestion with trypsin, a time-consuming step that 
      may be inadequate for covering certain sequence regions containing important 
      post-translational modifications (PTMs). RESULTS: In this study, we present an 
      in-line enzymatic digestion strategy for the targeted bottom-up analysis of 
      alpha-synuclein (alpha-syn), which is a protein biomarker of Parkinson's disease (PD). 
      The method is based on in-capillary enzymatic digestion capillary 
      electrophoresis-mass spectrometry (ICD-CE-MS) with endoproteinase GluC as an 
      alternative to trypsin, which fails to map Ser-129, a phosphorylated site in PD. 
      The method was optimized and validated using a recombinant alpha-syn standard, 
      focusing on the detection of potentially phosphorylated peptides. Under optimized 
      conditions, the method was repeatable at 100 mug mL(-1) of digested protein, 
      yielding percent relative standard deviation (%RSD) values of 3.4 % for peak area 
      and 7.2 % for migration time (n = 5) for the (124-131) alpha-syn peptide. Linearity 
      between 25 and 200 mug mL(-1) (R(2) > 0.995) was satisfactory, with a limit of 
      detection of 15 mug mL(-1) and a protein sequence coverage of 77.1 %. The method 
      was successfully applied to analyze endogenous alpha-syn in human blood. 
      SIGNIFICANCE: ICD-CE-MS with GluC extends the scope of targeted bottom-up 
      proteomics beyond trypsin-based workflows, offering a straightforward and fully 
      integrated platform for the sensitive targeted bottom-up analysis of challenging 
      protein biomarkers in biological fluids. This strategy can also be applied to the 
      analysis of protein biopharmaceuticals.
